Here is a summary of the last 60 minutes of the session:

The session covered several key Kubernetes concepts with practical demonstrations:

Deployment vs Replica Set: Vikas explained that a Replica Set creates multiple pods but has no manager to monitor them. If a pod goes down, no one automatically recreates it. Deployment, on the other hand, manages pods and maintains the desired state. If a pod gets deleted, deployment automatically creates a new one. Students were advised to focus on Deployment and avoid using Replica Set terminology in interviews.

Scaling/Editing Deployment: Vikas demonstrated how to edit a deployment using "kubectl edit deploy" command to change the number of replicas. He showed that changes are applied immediately and verified using "kubectl get pod".

Persistent Volume (PV) and Persistent Volume Claim (PVC): Using Netflix as an example, Vikas explained that PV is the actual data (like a movie), and PVC is when an application or user claims/uses that data. When a user watches a movie, a container is created and the volume is claimed. When the movie is completed, the container is released.

Stateful Set: When different applications (Android, iOS, TV, mobile) use the same data but in different formats, Stateful Set is used instead of Deployment.

Secrets and ConfigMap: Secrets store sensitive information like passwords and tokens. ConfigMap stores non-sensitive configuration information used to connect Kubernetes resources like pods and persistent volumes.

Jobs and Cron Jobs: Jobs are used to perform specific tasks at a particular time.

Practical Exercise - Jenkins Deployment: Vikas ran a Jenkins pod using the command "kubectl run jenkins --image=jenkins/jenkins:lts" and asked students to expose it as a Load Balancer on port 8080, similar to what was done previously with NGINX.

Connecting to Cluster: Vikas demonstrated how to connect to a GKE cluster using the gcloud command, which generates a kubeconfig file. He also mentioned that this can be done from a local machine by installing the Gcloud SDK.


Here is a summary of the last 10 minutes of the session:

The instructor was demonstrating how to deploy Jenkins on Kubernetes. He created a Jenkins pod using the kubectl run command with the Jenkins LTS image. After the pod was created, he asked students to expose it as a load balancer on port 8080, similar to what they had done earlier with NGINX.

He explained the three types of service exposure in Kubernetes:

Load Balancer - for internet-facing access, gives a public external IP so anyone can access it.
Node Port - exposes the application only within the same node, so only applications on the same node can communicate.
Cluster IP - exposes the application across the entire cluster, meaning all pods and services within the cluster can communicate with it, regardless of which node they are on.
Students like Sanket, Bala, Mandhir, and Rajiv successfully deployed Jenkins and got it running. The instructor verified their work by checking the external IP via the kubectl get svc command.

There was also a brief discussion about Kubernetes Secrets. One student mentioned that Kubernetes secrets are base64 encoded and can be easily decoded, raising a security concern. The instructor acknowledged this and mentioned that tools like HashiCorp Vault can be used as a more secure alternative, but stayed focused on the Kubernetes-native secret approach for the class.

The session then moved toward a discussion about unlocking Jenkins, where the instructor guided students on how to access the Jenkins container and get the initial unlock password to complete the Jenkins setup on Kubernetes.
Here are the practical commands used in the session:

Connect to GKE cluster:
gcloud container clusters get-credentials --project

Get nodes:
kubectl get nodes

Create a deployment:
kubectl create deployment nginx-deploy --image=nginx

Get deployments:
kubectl get deploy

Get pods:
kubectl get pod

Delete a pod:
kubectl delete pod

Edit a deployment (to change replicas or config):
kubectl edit deploy
Then change the replicas value, save with Escape + :wq + Enter

Get deployment in YAML format:
kubectl get deploy -o yaml

Run a Jenkins pod:
kubectl run jenkins --image=jenkins/jenkins:lts

Execute command inside a pod (to get Jenkins unlock password):
kubectl exec -it

Get services (to find load balancer IP):
kubectl get svc

Expose a pod as a load balancer service on port 8080:
This was done via UI (Workloads > Select pod > Expose > Choose Load Balancer > Port 8080), but can also be done via command:
kubectl expose pod jenkins --type=LoadBalancer --port=8080

Get all resources:
kubectl get all

Clear terminal:
clear

These commands cover cluster connection, deployment creation, scaling, editing, pod access, and service exposure as practiced in the session.
